= Falsifiability — Technical =

== Logical asymmetry ==
A universal law:

<pre>
∀x (P(x) → Q(x))
</pre>

is falsified by any instance <code>a</code> such that:

<pre>
P(a) ∧ ¬Q(a)
</pre>

This asymmetry supports Popper's emphasis on potential falsifiers.

Finite confirming instances do not deductively entail an unrestricted universal generalization.

== Basic statements ==
In Popper's framework, empirical theories conflict with classes of possible "basic statements."

A theory is more testable when it excludes a larger class of possible observations.

Logical falsifiability is therefore a relation between the theory and a language/model of possible observation statements.

== Auxiliary hypotheses ==
Empirical prediction normally has a structure such as:

<pre>
Theory T
∧ auxiliary assumptions A
∧ initial conditions I
∧ measurement model M
→ prediction P
</pre>

Observed <code>¬P</code> refutes the conjunction, not logically T alone.

Methodological falsification requires deciding which part of the conjunction to revise.

== Probabilistic hypotheses ==
For stochastic H:

<pre>
P(E | H) > 0
</pre>

even low-probability E is logically compatible with H.

Statistical tests therefore use rejection regions, likelihood ratios, Bayes factors, posterior distributions, or other inferential criteria rather than strict contradiction from one event.

== Severe testing ==
A test is informative when the tested hypothesis had a substantial chance of failing under relevant alternatives or when passing would be difficult if the claim were false.

This intuition connects Popperian risk-taking with later statistical and methodological ideas of severe tests, though formal theories differ.

== Ad hoc modification ==
Adding auxiliary assumptions after a failed prediction is not automatically illegitimate.

A modification can be scientifically productive when it:
* has independent motivation;
* generates new predictions;
* increases explanatory integration;
* is itself testable.

Purely protective modifications that only shield a theory from any conceivable refutation reduce testability.

